<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="taylor-apps-complete">
    <title>Applications of Taylor Series (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We've seen examples of series representations of functions. We'll continue to see how new 
                        representations can be obtained from old, as well as some examples where series representations 
                        are useful in application and computation.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Differentiation and Integration</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If 
                        <md>
                            f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x-a)^n
                        </md>
                        with radius of convergence <m>R</m>, then
                         
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        we can compute <m>f'(x)</m> and <m>\int f(x) \quad dx</m> by differentiating and integrating 
                        the series term-by-term. The resulting series' inherit the same radius of convergence <m>R</m> as the
                        original series.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="5in">
                <title>Examples: Differentiation and Integration</title>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\cos(x)</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Cosine is the derivative of sine. Therefore,
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\cos(x) \amp=\frac{d}{dx}\left(\sin(x)\right) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \frac{d}{dx}\left(\sum_{n=0}^\infty (-1)^n \frac{x^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}\right) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \sum_{n=0}^\infty (-1)^n \frac{x^{2n}}{(2n)!}  </mrow>
                            </md>,
                            and <m>R=\infty</m>.
                            
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\ln(1+x)</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            This is an antiderivative of <m>\frac{1}{1+x}</m>, therefore
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\ln(1+x) \amp= \int \frac{1}{1+x} \quad dx </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \int \sum_{n=0}^\infty (-1)^n x^n \quad dx </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{(-1)^n}{n+1} x^{n+1}  + C </mrow>
                            </md>
                            Since <m>\ln(1+0) = 0</m>, we must have that <m>C=0</m>. Also, <m>R = 1</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                
                
            </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
                <title>Approximating Integrals</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        We've seen that computing integrals can be a challenge, and we've already seen some numerical 
                        techniques for approximating integrals. Series representations give us yet another approach:
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Compute <m>\displaystyle \int e^{-x^2} \quad dx</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\displaystyle \int e^{-x^2} \quad dx \amp= \int \sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{(-x^2)^n}{n!} \quad dx </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{(-1)^nx^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)n!} +C</mrow>
                            </md>
                            We can <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/5pwvsnhpzr">visualize the first several terms of this function</url> with technology such as Desmos.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>

                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            Estimate <m>\displaystyle \int_{-2}^2 e^{-x^2} \quad dx</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>F(x)</m> be the antiderivative of <m>e^{-x^2}</m> (with <m>C=0</m>) in series form found above. Then
                            <md>
                                <mrow> \int_{-2}^2 e^{-x^2} \quad dx \amp= F(2) - F(-2) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{(-1)^n(2)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)n!} - \sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{(-1)^n(-2)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)n!}  </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp\approx \sum_{n=0}^{100} \frac{(-1)^n(2)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)n!} - \sum_{n=0}^{100} \frac{(-1)^n(-2)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)n!}</mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp\approx 1.764 </mrow>
                            </md>.
                            
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
              
                
            </exercise>
</page>

   

       
    </worksheet>
